  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-hermes-panel",
      "text": "[中文](README.zh.md) | English\n\nA [Hermes](https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent) panel for\n[DSH](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness), in the right sidebar: start and stop the\ngateway and dashboard, watch the dashboard beside the chat, and bridge DSH to Hermes.\n\nFor the native Hermes agent (`hermes-agent`). Not for `hermes-web-ui` / Ekko Studio."
    },
    {
      "section": "Panel",
      "text": "Open it from the ⊕ list in the right sidebar → **Open Hermes**. The strip on top switches between\nthree views and carries start/stop, restart and open-in-new-tab:\n\n- **Dashboard** — the Hermes dashboard in an iframe, conversation on the left.\n- **Record** — the Hermes ↔ DSH exchange, read from DSH's own session logs.\n- **Setup** — health check, update, the DSH bridge, and the shared workspace."
    },
    {
      "section": "Panel",
      "text": "Health and update run the `official-web` distribution's own PowerShell scripts. An upstream pip\ninstall has neither, so those two buttons are disabled there and the panel says why. Everything\nelse works on any install."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web add @yang3333/dsh-hermes-panel\n```\n\nThen restart `dsh`: the host half is a Node module, and a page refresh does not reload it.\n\nNeeds DSH `>= 0.2.0-rc.2` and an existing Hermes install — the plugin does not install Hermes, it\nfinds it or is told where it is. Windows only."
    },
    {
      "section": "Settings",
      "text": "Two directories, both optional. Empty means a default under the DSH home.\n\n- **Runtime** — the Python environment Hermes runs in. Default `<dsh home>/hermes-panel/runtime`.\n- **Data directory** — `HERMES_HOME`: profiles, sessions, `config.yaml`. Default\n  `<dsh home>/hermes-panel/home`.\n\nThe rest: `dashboardPort` (0 = read from `.env`, else 9150), `sharedWorkspace`, `manageGateway`,\n`autostart`.\n\nChange them in the **Plugins** panel → **Official** → **Hermes Panel settings**."
    },
    {
      "section": "Bridge",
      "text": "With the bridge open, the panel writes the Hermes side (`mcp_servers.dsh`) into `config.yaml` and\nchecks that each profile's `@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client` matches the running DSH. When one does not,\nit prints the command that fixes it."
    },
    {
      "section": "Security",
      "text": "The HTTP routes are loopback-only and check `Origin` and `Sec-Fetch-Site`. `.env` parsing keeps only\n`HERMES_HOME`, `VENV`, `HERMES_EXE` and `DASHBOARD_PORT`; no other value is stored. Do not bind DSH\nto `0.0.0.0` while the plugin is enabled."
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "```sh\nnpm test          # hermetic; safe to run while Hermes is running\nnpm run test:e2e  # drives the real scripts and a real dashboard\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "The suite lives in `verify/`, one file per area, each with a comment saying what regression it\nguards. `verify/smoke.mjs` loads the host half with an empty config, asserts it registers 17 routes\nand that activation spawns nothing, then calls the status route with no Hermes installed.\n`verify/config.mjs` checks that the config loads with all 6 fields volatile, that a value saved after\nactivation is what the panel reads, and that `.env` parsing keeps only the four whitelisted keys."
    },
    {
      "section": "License",
      "text": "MIT"
    }
  ],
